#{[17592186275305 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}3{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186275306 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{4}5{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186275307 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186275317 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186275319 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186275320 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186275322 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡y}\n$$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186275323 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186275325 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$"] [17592186275339 :question/problem "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [17592186275340 :question/problem "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [17592186275341 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=A,\n$$ what is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [17592186275342 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=A,\n$$ what is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [17592186275343 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{8}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=A,\n$$ what is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-8}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [17592186275344 :question/problem "![](images/36/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ determine the value of $g(2).$"] [17592186275345 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a3b8289936cd2597ac1252e2b28987f245a7c4e88dcf3677e1ba3626460688b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ determine the value of $g(3).$"] [17592186275362 :question/problem "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [17592186275368 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-8y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(8y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{8},\n$$ $x=4,$ and $y=2,$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot (4))-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275379 :question/problem "Consider the section of the parabola $3{x}^{2}+4y=1$ that lies in the fourth quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ on the parabola at the point where $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point."] [17592186275379 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{x}^{2}+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6x)+\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot (4) & =0 \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation of  the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4y & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the fourth quadrant.  \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275407 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{3{y}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186275423 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}.\n$$"] [17592186275450 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}\n$$ for $x\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186275457 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡A}-\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡A}=\n$$"] [17592186275472 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [17592186275476 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [17592186278178 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [17592186278179 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” only."] [17592186278180 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” only."] [17592186278181 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ only."] [17592186278182 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” only."] [17592186278186 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}k(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =k{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =k([\\frac{9}{2}+3]-[\\frac{4}{2}+2]) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{9}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{2}k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186278188 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2k}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{25}{2k}-\\frac{9}{2k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$"] [17592186278190 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(k-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[kx-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-[-k-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =k+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186278191 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$"] [17592186278223 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{-4}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186278270 :question/problem "Given a data set with $12$ data points and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=84,\n$$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$"] [17592186278270 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=84,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 84=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278292 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186278293 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ 4u+6v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186278294 :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $$\n\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\n$$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=\\pi $ only."] [17592186278295 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.\n$$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186278295 :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $$\n\\displaystyle f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.\n$$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=-1$; $y=1$ only."] [17592186278302 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x/2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x/2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & =2({e}^{0}-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$” only."] [17592186278303 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” only."] [17592186278304 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{1-2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” only."] [17592186278305 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [17592186278307 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}$ on the interval $x\\ge -2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{6(x+3)^{6}}\\,{|}_{-2}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$"] [17592186278308 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}}$ on the interval $x\\ge 2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(2x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{2(2x-1)}\\,{|}_{2}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$"] [17592186278309 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{1-2x}$ on the interval $x\\ge 1,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{2}{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{2e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{2e}=1\\,⟹\\,k=2e.\n$$"] [17592186278310 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{3x+2}$ on the interval $x\\le 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{3x+2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k{e}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k{e}^{2}}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186278311 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{2-x}$ on the interval $x\\ge -1,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x=-k{e}^{2-x}\\,{|}_{-1}^{\\infty }=k{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk{e}^{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k={e}^{-3}.\n$$"] [17592186278344 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c8/c8ad4cf64e9ea17b225373d8ed238c8588c0b6f45577d2ca15b22ede5e34d7d8.png)"] [17592186278345 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png)"] [17592186278350 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the third quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{9},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png)"] [17592186278355 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$” is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis” only."] [17592186278356 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b836799bc512db70d2ce5fcfe70db2af3b2572d584d317fa5ed515b363a11ddc.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “One endpoint of the image is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, and the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $5$ centered at $O.$” is false.\n\n- When $u=0,$ we obtain the point $(0,\\,0),$ which is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane.\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{5},$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to $O$ is $\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSo, one endpoint of the segment is the origin $O$ of the $xy$-plane, while the other endpoint lies in the circle with radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis” only."] [17592186278358 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa5b04b98d3796656fca912d8dc1732ea7e12bab9083f8ca543ad5fd4fdb10ff.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186278359 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdd5f0c58228b47a0bbba77daf2b426ce0247e71259e2b5af1a4a80fa833a036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186278360 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186278366 :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above."] [17592186278369 :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186278370 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186278371 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186278374 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4da25a7fcce468774aafcf2c1dbf7b4ef65089194d0330b1159d18a9a8f4bfe9.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186278379 :question/problem "![](images/ae/aed70cc2e5495fcfd9f42ff5d79c3371fb17b62e12ab1909c7738071e59f1a23.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=x+2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186278384 :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above."] [17592186278385 :question/problem "![](images/63/636c9e587d6cbb9fcf9b24d068520330d5365cf80a2800bdd8244698512be3c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $5,$ as shown above."] [17592186278386 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$” is true, while statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$” is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](images/65/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$” only."] [17592186278388 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cd21fbe460f530f08bc8e85f407f9d6ac49b263d3a5361af093a21cd1ca8d11.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is a half annulus enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above."] [17592186278389 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$” and statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$” are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](images/ed/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186278403 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true, while statement $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$ is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$” is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\,:\\\\\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186278418 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y,z)=⟨-\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{2},\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2},\\,z-1⟩\n$$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x-2y+1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*"] [17592186278435 :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂x}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂x}{∂z}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{3}+{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})=3{y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})={e}^{z},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂x}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂y}{∂z}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}+({e}^{z})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$"] [17592186278444 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2-y-z$” and “${f}_{1}(y,z)=2+y-z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=5$” are false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=2+y-z.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2af4029bd979d250ed15f351ef7f14d3ae688806db4ffec199a24b3591dadac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186278445 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z$” is true, while statement “${f}_{1}(x,z)=4-z$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4$” is false. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1046e312a3783dfc3253646f8a71ae226a173ac882a6305850bdd81aed9dd7e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ “${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z$” only."] [17592186278447 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(x,z)=z-\\frac{x}{3}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3$” is false, while statement “${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z$” is true. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z.$\n\n![](images/88/88b5fde383f094436dd94adefa7912f1a194319739fdb0b8a6a9a74693362219.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ “${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=3+\\frac{x}{3}-z$” only."] [17592186278451 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{-1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [17592186278452 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [17592186278453 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186278454 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}-2z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [17592186278456 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $$\n\\displaystyle R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\n$$"] [17592186278456 :question/worked-solution "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $$\n\\displaystyle R=[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]\\times [0,1]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5]\n$$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{y{e}^{z}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot y\\cdot {e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{\\sqrt{2}/4}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278457 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $$\n\\displaystyle R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{e}^{2}\\le y\\le {e}^{5},\\,0\\le z\\le 4}.\n$$"] [17592186278457 :question/worked-solution "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $$\n\\displaystyle R=[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]\\times [{e}^{2},{e}^{5}]\\times [0,4]\n$$ is a rectangular box, and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{z{sec}^{2}⁡x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z & =[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{5}}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}+1)\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}+1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =24(\\sqrt{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278460 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $$\n\\displaystyle R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,5\\le z\\le 7}.\n$$"] [17592186278460 :question/worked-solution "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $$\n\\displaystyle R=[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [0,1]\\times [5,7]\n$$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{3}^{y-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{z-4}\\,\\text{d}z & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{3}^{y-1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-4)]_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =(-0+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278466 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{e}{e}^{-z}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=\n$$"] [17592186278467 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx=\n$$"] [17592186278472 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{y}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [17592186278473 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{y}{z})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186278474 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{z}({y}^{2}-x)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=\n$$"] [17592186278476 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-y)^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [17592186278482 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=\n$$"] [17592186278485 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}z}\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186278489 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\frac{x}{z}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{z}\\right)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx=\n$$"] [17592186278494 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V,\\,\n$$\n\nover the box $$\n\\displaystyle R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$"] [17592186278494 :question/worked-solution "First, we express our triple integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $$\n\\displaystyle R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\times [\\frac{1}{2},1]\n$$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi }\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{z}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+1)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (-1+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278496 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z$” is true and statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=4+y-2z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4$” is false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf8e141067fb6f675933f52e465114d8909d951f6ac85fb09c8dda7efbc2f38.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=4-y+2z$” only."] [17592186278502 :question/problem "By integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x,$ evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y+z)\\,dV\n$$\n\nover the rectangular box $$\n\\displaystyle R={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\pi }.\n$$"] [17592186278508 :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between  the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\n$$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186278508 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and *above* by $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{4}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le \\underbrace{\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\n$$” is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\n$$ and the plane $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](images/46/46757c2bf93af4916afbd019f0faed2eef25245e987acbeff7eafb3e0ca7cd8c.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le \\underbrace{4\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement “${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}$” is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-3}_{a}\\le y\\le \\underbrace{3}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement “$a=-3$ and $b=3$” is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\n$$”; “$a=-3$ and $b=3$” only."] [17592186278538 :question/problem "![](images/33/330b550b31987e29aceb4b2a253e26ebec470692a6a0144f64725801f2df2b7d.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}x\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above. \n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186278539 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d6a1a5ca848d7563763a3384855daf13031c808852efa541a73e2ef3126c6ed.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}4xy\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above. \n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186278540 :question/problem "![](images/89/89fba52c9a48d4f8f2ddac0b060c3ffab7e3ba4f6652124f4a9ac6ba39a579e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}(3x-1)\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186278541 :question/problem "![](images/00/00e09ca44feb1fcfa5d3ece010a858dbd98efe57985b0f8d5210f9d98a02f498.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186278542 :question/problem "![](images/91/91e8b39eb1bf181ceaf5b33b25fbb5ed0fa3ca7f7c1f0bb830c3337f142eb1dd.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186278552 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcc7c78752700a17970da69c3809a8c621aaf23580ab0c3329335083858bd019.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [17592186278567 :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5690bff5819d72cc9d49016c119dc79f62f4d766c142a43a89df196ce9f2335.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186278569 :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1f3493cd1ff0bee115bf7c311504a2b8a9bbd38dd35adae96c9f7abcae40ae1.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [17592186278576 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 3}$ only."] [17592186278577 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186278578 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 8,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186278579 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 1}$; $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2x-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}.\n$$"] [17592186278581 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-3<x<3,\\\\\\,0<y<|2x|\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,1<y<3-{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186278582 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0,\\\\\\,0<y<\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-2<x<2,\\\\\\,0<y<{x}^{2}+1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186281320 :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$ intersects the line $\\sqrt{3}x+y=0.$"] [17592186281322 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d148f8e38e2ad3bc7b1ca97a15b69c23ce315bc67ac2738009ca12f0fd7aef72.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{yz}{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281323 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3fe3ec24f8f8006f22bde8ba2dc39fd3cd3f73668b06f8ea4a74e4ed5206a193.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{10-2y-z}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281324 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca49b92d602d04a73dc7ab9936d601654bd293ce3fb7950372ac86155875aca3.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{6-y+3z}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281350 :question/problem "![](images/75/7523d27fa1aad56d67cb40a47715cc42d1f371c209ab4a9e5aef15413bc69490.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281351 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6d469b24061f0f7284691b2e2f26a2c28d9557ab72a1c8ba3cad93eb65dde91.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}2{z}^{2}\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281355 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/70/70450f3fc9e9971a76e4458c5e020c1f264e739913fbb24d3e6ff22359542316.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\n$$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$"] [17592186281358 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1e4a1e39c85a037f3f4f49910b651300363ef34bee969464f5e711aa00986e3.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}4{x}^{2}z\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281365 :question/problem "![](images/79/79a1c7b23b395aeb8653c8f83b52b5a3271c87d5784dcb27f4e1e47b5642f2c4.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}x\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281383 :question/problem "![](images/93/93df9649232a5985eaefebd7180c8f40c6608d51fe8b993cf3da9a8473eb4612.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}xz\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186281405 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+2v+1 \\\\ 2u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram."] [17592186281414 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-3v+2 \\\\ 4v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-3v \\\\ 4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."] [17592186281415 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2v+1 \\\\ 2u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2v \\\\ 2u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."] [17592186281417 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane."] [17592186281418 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [17592186281419 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane."] [17592186281420 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [17592186281423 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*"] [17592186281429 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186281430 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*"] [17592186281439 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186281440 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*"] [17592186281441 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*"] [17592186281443 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*"] [17592186281480 :question/problem "![](images/38/380b44a8baf8ed15eb3eb4e66e77b820f5bc51a73be840526db67d96f6d763ed.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\frac{1}{(1+{x}^{2})(1-x)}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\n$$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*"] [17592186281481 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{\\pi }^{2}/4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{z}{y}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz=\n$$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*"] [17592186281491 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{y}xyz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx=\n$$"] [17592186281514 :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j+\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,k\n$$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186281532 :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation, $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretching, and $R$ be the matrix that represents the reflection. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $RQP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RQP & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle a+b=(-3)+1=-2.\n$$"] [17592186281539 :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1-2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y &=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1+2\\lambda ) &=1 \\\\ y(1-4\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1+\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}),(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\pm \\frac{2}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(-1,0)$ and its minimum value is $-2.$"] [17592186281561 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{64}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186281564 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,a=1,\\,b=e.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{e-1}{1}=e-1.\n$$"] [17592186281565 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{15}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$"] [17592186281566 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$"] [17592186281567 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{15}{32}\\right)}=\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186281570 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot (8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8x-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{48}{5}\\right)}{12}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186281619 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)}=2.\n$$"] [17592186281620 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{{e}^{-y}}^{1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x={e}^{-y}}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}0-(-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$"] [17592186281650 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =3, & \\,{m}_{2} & =6, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, & \\,{m}_{4} & =5, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2.5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =1, & \\,{x}_{4} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+6+1+5 \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 2.5+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6+15+1+20 \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{30}{15}=2.\n$$"] [17592186281651 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =5, & \\,{m}_{2} & =1, & \\,{m}_{3} & =2, & \\,{m}_{4} & =4, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =4, & \\,{x}_{3} & =0.5, & \\,{x}_{4} & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =5+1+2+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0.5+4\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =5+4+1+6 \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{16}{12}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186281655 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =7, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =7+4+1 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7+8+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186281656 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =3, & \\,{m}_{3} & =4, & \\,{m}_{4} & =5, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3, & \\,{x}_{2} & =-2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =-0.5, & \\,{x}_{4} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+4+5 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot (-0.5)+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6-6-2+10 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{-4}{14}=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186281657 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=1,\\,{m}_{3}=7.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+1+7 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6+1+28 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{23}{10}.\n$$"] [17592186281658 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=1,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=6.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-4,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =1+3+6 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4-6+18 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186281659 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=6,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =6+2+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-18+4+16 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{2}{12}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"] [17592186281660 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=4,\\,{y}_{2}=2,\\,{y}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8+6+2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{16}{6}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186281661 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=3,\\,{m}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=3,\\,{y}_{2}=5,\\,{y}_{3}=2,\\,{y}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+2+3+1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3}+{m}_{4}{y}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6+10+6+4 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{26}{8}=\\frac{13}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186281664 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/a0/a042919f73d668cac20ead2752c689e57880f60bc096747f41cec3aeb3ed62ca.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$"] [17592186281665 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b6/b6e8af58b8e7ba5f909d226b895bb39b3fc94e53686e4718eb32c9bb4ee61c08.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot [y]_{y=0}^{y={x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186281666 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/30/3097e540292efd601743ba8de24acffc3b51469e77561b2bccbd799d1125e1ed.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14{y}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{y}^{4}}{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186284388 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186284389 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,0).$"] [17592186284389 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+6x-4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4{y}^{3}-4x+8y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=6x+6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=12{y}^{2}+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x+6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12{y}^{2}+8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=12\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=80.\n$$"] [17592186284390 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$"] [17592186284390 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$"] [17592186284391 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\pi ).$"] [17592186284391 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,\\pi ),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\pi  & \\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,\\pi )=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-\\pi \\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$"] [17592186284415 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4be9362c014a7b8f0d0708ca5d7ea32a1b5bb13d48e2e1704341d04b89f9566a.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{4}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [17592186284454 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2}=1?\n$$"] [17592186284457 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.\n$$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"] [17592186284458 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle y+1=\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{3}.\n$$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"] [17592186284459 :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z+4=\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186284460 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $z$-axis."] [17592186284463 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1,\n$$ find its $x$-intercepts."] [17592186284464 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z+2=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{3}?\n$$"] [17592186284466 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{5}-(z-1)^{2}=-1,\n$$ find its $y$-intercepts."] [17592186284467 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=-1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186284472 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5}=1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186284475 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186284476 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=-1?\n$$"] [17592186284478 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z+3)^{2}}{12}=-1\n$$ and the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?"] [17592186284479 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+(z-2)^{2}=-1\n$$ and the plane $z=3$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?"] [17592186284480 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{7}=1?\n$$"] [17592186284481 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1?\n$$"] [17592186284489 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?\n$$"] [17592186284501 :question/problem "Identify the surface $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186284538 :question/problem "Given the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\n$$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k.$"] [17592186284540 :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$"] [17592186284540 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$"] [17592186284541 :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $$\n\\displaystyle (x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$"] [17592186284542 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).\n$$"] [17592186284544 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?\n$$"] [17592186284544 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186284545 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?\n$$"] [17592186284545 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186284546 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x}\\,i+\\frac{z}{y}\\,j+k\n$$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$"] [17592186284547 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}\\,i+\\sqrt{y}\\,j+z\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,2].$"] [17592186284550 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"] [17592186284551 :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes."] [17592186284589 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186284590 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 1?$"] [17592186284593 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(2{x}^{2}-16)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [17592186284601 :question/problem "What is the linear transformation $T$ whose standard matrix is \n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186284612 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=6-2y$” is false, while statement “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” is true. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}.$\n\n![](images/48/487b57182e190cbacf3b1ad30f55d227b59fb470e8b5609ad72327d229e53e45.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}D=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(y,z)\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\\\\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ “${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$” only."] [17592186284623 :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt[3]{(3-4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186284627 :question/problem "For the curve $$\n\\displaystyle y=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},\n$$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis."] [17592186284627 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle y=2{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$"] [17592186284628 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{2}{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186284629 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ hence $c=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4} & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{5}{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186284633 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{{(2-x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186284654 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2}?\n$$"] [17592186284654 :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a6/a67681a546ecd949fd8cc3be1d5d0647a3cbf88c8809bdc2c620f0d42cb89c8b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2},\n$$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7dbfba1ee7e66fbc15858f668aacc27db4bdeebb3a7e28f243f2dc62216dda57.png)"] [17592186284660 :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186284661 :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186284675 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{({x}^{3}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186284676 :question/problem "Evaluate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2-{x}^{3})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186284678 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}).\n$$"] [17592186284685 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$"] [17592186284685 :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186284693 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle {[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)]}^{16}.\n$$"] [17592186284745 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-cos⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [17592186284746 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+8)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{3}=\\frac{(z-7)^{2}}{7},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [17592186284772 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8810b09e23c88d5833b43804cf62f08ce91dfa426a0d9363a579ecff2bca715.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186284774 :question/problem "![](images/59/59ef310974793486d22fd5a8967ecb9a2821bf4cd0c1feec1d2094ead812fd38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186284781 :question/problem "![](images/3b/3bd3e39caa4db8d65502ffafd876cc68c54016461b6905ef5061608aed44d8d4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment, an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(-2,-2)$ with radius $1,$ and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(3,2)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186284782 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186287675 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [17592186287683 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(2{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$"] [17592186287708 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{3x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x+1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$"] [17592186287714 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$"] [17592186287821 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3y & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & y & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n is $81,$ find all the possible values of $y.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [17592186287824 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-30,$ find the value of $k.$"] [17592186287878 :question/problem "Let ${X}_{i}={0,i,2i},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=\n$$"] [17592186287879 :question/problem "Let ${S}_{i}=[0+\\frac{1}{i},1],$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=\n$$"] [17592186287882 :question/problem "Let ${Y}_{i}=[-i,0],$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=3}^{\\infty }{Y}_{i}=\n$$"] [17592186287883 :question/problem "Let ${S}_{i}=[1+\\frac{1}{i},2],$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=\n$$"] [17592186287883 :question/worked-solution "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }$ means that our set of indexes is \n${5,6,7…}.$ \n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=5,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{5}=[1+\\frac{1}{5},2]=[\\frac{6}{5},2]\n$$\n\n- if $i=6,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{6}=[1+\\frac{1}{6},2]=[\\frac{7}{6},2]\n$$\n\n- if $i=7,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{7}=[1+\\frac{1}{7},2]=[\\frac{8}{7},2]\n$$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals approaches $1$ but never reaches it, and\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals equals $2$ for all the sets.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=5}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}=(1,2].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186287927 :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186287944 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186287945 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-0.25}^{0.25}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-12{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186287946 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/5}\\frac{20}{3+75{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186287952 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{3+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186287978 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}(3z+2)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [17592186287987 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [17592186287996 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186288000 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186288006 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186288010 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."] [17592186288011 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186288013 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186288016 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186288018 :question/problem "![](images/59/59704c6b8e643337113ab99b22eaeaac8d59ac3c15042409e8a0a8eac6ee54fd.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y={e}^{t}\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?"] [17592186290265 :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186290275 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186290276 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186290277 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290281 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290291 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}{4}^{{x}^{3}/5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186290298 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?"] [17592186290299 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\n$$\nfrom $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?"] [17592186290300 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?"] [17592186290333 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=x+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{55{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"] [17592186290336 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290340 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4csc⁡xcot⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290366 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290380 :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186290380 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{1}{10},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =4(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1)^{3}(-1-2)=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1)^{3}(1-2)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =(3)^{3}(3-2)=27>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$” only."] [17592186290382 :question/problem "Consider the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$ Given that ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186290382 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={x}^{7}-\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=42{x}^{5}-70{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =42(0)^{5}-70(0)^{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{6}-14{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{5}(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =7(-1)^{5}(-1-2)=21>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =7(1)^{5}(1-2)=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =7(3)^{5}(3-2)=7\\cdot {3}^{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$” only."] [17592186290433 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $$\n\\displaystyle f\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},\n$$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$”; “The global minimum value is $0$” only.\n\n![](images/53/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)"] [17592186290437 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290438 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290455 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186290461 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [17592186290504 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290507 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [17592186290508 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186290509 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int z{e}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [17592186290514 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x({x}^{2}-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186290518 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186290526 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/b8/b81b4602d221eb04aab76b0ed8853ab75a5c3cf93e76cc489344bc15133c4daf.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\n$$"] [17592186290531 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290531 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{5}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={5}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{{5}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290533 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290545 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [17592186290550 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [17592186290558 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}=\n$$"] [17592186290559 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\n$$"] [17592186290560 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [17592186290562 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle 8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\right)=\n$$"] [17592186290563 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=4$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (10+23+17+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 68 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(10-17)^{2}+(23-17)^{2}+(17-17)^{2}+(18-17)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[49+36+0+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{86}{4} \\\\  & =21.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186290576 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186290577 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186290585 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [17592186290586 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290591 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290599 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186290600 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186292544 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind ${2}^{D}.$\n\n| ${2}^{D}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186292545 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi D}{6}\\right).$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi D}{6}\\right)=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186292546 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\pi \\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $\\operatorname{sin}⁡D.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡D=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |"] [17592186292552 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D.$\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡D=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186292610 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186292630 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=4$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (14+21+13+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(14-16)^{2}+(21-16)^{2}+(13-16)^{2}+(16-16)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4+25+9+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{4} \\\\  & =9.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292631 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (1-6-1+3-11-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(1-(-3))^{2}+(-6-(-3))^{2}+(-1-(-3))^{2}+(3-(-3))^{2}+(-11-(-3))^{2}+(-4-(-3))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[16+9+4+36+64+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{130}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292632 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=64,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (64) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=768,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 768 \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =96-{(8)}^{2} \\\\  & =96-64 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292633 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=63,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (63) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=686,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 686 \\\\  & =98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =98-{(9)}^{2} \\\\  & =98-81 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292634 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=104,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (104) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=1448,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 1448 \\\\  & =181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =181-{(13)}^{2} \\\\  & =181-169 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292635 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=12$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=-180,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-180) \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=3564,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 3564 \\\\  & =297.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =297-{(-15)}^{2} \\\\  & =297-225 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292636 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=-112,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (-112) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=1736,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 1736 \\\\  & =217.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =217-{(-14)}^{2} \\\\  & =217-196 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292695 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{-1+\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{1-2x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186292697 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-6-\\sqrt{6}}^{-6+\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{-12-x(x+12)}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186292698 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186292714 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}-1}-\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292715 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{B+2}-\\frac{1}{{B}^{2}+2B}=\\frac{2}{B}.\n$$\n\n$B=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292731 :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{2x-3}{3x-2}\\ge 0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle )⋃[\n$$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$).$"] [17592186292733 :question/problem "The values of $x$ that satisfy the inequality $\\frac{(x-2)(x-1)}{x+3}>0$ lie in the interval\n\n$\\,x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle )⋃(\n$$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty ).$"] [17592186292738 :question/problem "Using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\n$$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292739 :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{45}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,1+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$"] [17592186292784 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3 & =3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186292802 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle z=6\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right))\n$$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$"] [17592186292807 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\n$$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$"] [17592186292846 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nthen\n\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [17592186292847 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2?\n$$"] [17592186292847 :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/27/27e33d04bccb0136877dce919662aa5f78bb8315e6b2931461419fc8bc1664bd.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/d9/d98570b1dc97924ccb9af8a252769f0873387b029dcd5f95be8be57942f2fb7a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}+2.\n$$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=2.$\n\n![](images/21/2192d5153baf0170024880585d956aec347caedc0f8ac1aeda56e9a225675863.png)"] [17592186292855 :question/problem "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 3 & y \\\\ 2 & x & z \\\\ 1 & w & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{12}$ of the element ${a}_{12}.$\n\n${M}_{12}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186292861 :question/problem "Calculate the product $D{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| $D\\cdot {I}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [17592186292862 :question/problem "Find the product $AB,$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{5} & -6 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186292865 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=2\\cdot -2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,6=2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,2=-1\\cdot -2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-3=-1\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186292869 :question/problem "Find $Ha$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$Ha=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [17592186292869 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\cdot 7+6\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle Ha=\\begin{bmatrix}34 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186292870 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & y \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}|=\n$$\n {{field-1}}"] [17592186292871 :question/problem "If \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & a \\\\ a & -b & a \\\\ 0 & 0 & a\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle r=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $Qr.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$Qr=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>"] [17592186292872 :question/problem "If\n    $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(C)=-14,$ determine the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292873 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3+p & 4p-{p}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5-p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=7,$ what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292885 :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292886 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=-k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186292887 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}"] [17592186292888 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292891 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+3y &=1 \\\\ x+4y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292921 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single roll of the game. On each roll in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If rolling gives an odd number ($1$, $3,$ or $5$), the player loses $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\n- If rolling gives an even number ($2$ or $4$), the player wins $$10,$ so we have $p=10.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \nP(-2)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(10)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n\n$$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $-2$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}E[p] & =(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+10\\cdot f(10) \\\\  & =(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.80.$"] [17592186292934 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3h+2}+\\frac{1}{5h}=\\frac{1}{h(3h+2)}.\n$$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292935 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2x}+\\frac{1}{3x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292936 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292937 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5s(s+1)}+\\frac{3}{5s}=\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186292938 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{R-1}-\\frac{2}{R-2}=\\frac{2}{{R}^{2}-3R+2}.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294828 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{a-3}-\\frac{10}{{a}^{2}-2a-3}=\\frac{2}{a+1}.\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294829 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{m+5}+\\frac{1}{m-2}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+3m-10}.\n$$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294830 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.\n$$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294831 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{h}-\\frac{3}{2h-3}=\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}-3h}.\n$$\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294839 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{y+2}=\\frac{7}{{y}^{2}-4}-\\frac{1}{y-2}.\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294840 :question/problem "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-25}=\\frac{1}{w-5}-\\frac{4}{w+5}.\n$$\n\n$w=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294844 :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-20 & =0 \\\\ 8x & =20 \\\\ x & =\\frac{20}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186294847 :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x-9 & =0 \\\\ 15x & =9 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{15} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186294850 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $q(x)=\\frac{4}{9x-k}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3})\\cup (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ),\n$$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [17592186294851 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{10}{kx+4}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),\n$$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [17592186294853 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+7}{bx+5}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{6})\\cup (-\\frac{5}{6},\\infty ),\n$$ then $b=${{field-1}}."] [17592186294855 :question/problem "If the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8x-9}{kx-2}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ),\n$$ then $k=${{field-1}}."] [17592186294863 :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c6e305056cc2128d2dffdbe4ae84cd21d7767dbfce67ca721a94cf24e54420d.png)\n\nGiven the sketch of a polynomial $p(x)$ above, find the solution to $p(x)>0.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)⋃(-2,$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle )⋃(\n$$\n{{field-4}}$,\\infty )$"] [17592186294869 :question/problem "Solve for $p$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p}=-\\frac{p-2}{6p}.\n$$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294882 :question/problem "Solve for $m$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m}=\\frac{5}{12m}.\n$$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294883 :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a+5}{a}+\\frac{2-a}{5a}=\\frac{7}{10a}.\n$$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294884 :question/problem "Solve for $f$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}=\\frac{f+2}{2f}.\n$$\n\n$f=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294885 :question/problem "Solve for $y$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}=\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294886 :question/problem "Solve for $n$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{n}.\n$$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186294897 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186294911 :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-17x & >-2{x}^{2}+45 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=6{x}^{2}-17x-45$ lies **above** the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-17x-45 & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{2}-27x)+(10x-45) & =0 \\\\ 3x(2x-9)+5(2x-9) & =0 \\\\ (3x+5)(2x-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ and $x=\\frac{9}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f73c9906e6b65d1ddbda91619c7ffbb77408faa0541e2bc16565e0127d334312.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-45>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x<-\\frac{5}{3}$ or $x>\\frac{9}{2}.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})⋃(\\frac{9}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186294916 :question/problem "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}=1\n$$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [17592186294936 :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186294954 :question/worked-solution "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & >49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >\\frac{49}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} \\\\ |x| & >\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{6}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},+\\infty ).\n$$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>\\frac{49}{36}$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{6})(x+\\frac{7}{6}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots are  $x=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}$   is positive, the parabola opens upward. Hence, the solution set to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots and does not include them:\n\n![](images/14/14702e7d185d38587cf10642c8a455d9d25a51d2a8eebbf7e0e54a79223ea56e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186294979 :question/problem "The center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ is located at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [17592186294984 :question/problem "From **left to right,** the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand \n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$"] [17592186294986 :question/problem "The $x$-intercept of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}=1\n$$ is$\\,(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$"] [17592186295008 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{49}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{7}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186295009 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{11}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186295010 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186295011 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+8)^{2}}{81}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186295048 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186295052 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186295058 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle ⋃[\n$$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$"] [17592186295215 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)(2x-1)(1-x)(3x-4)(x-5).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $x=5.$ These roots divide the number line into $6$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},1),\\,(1,\\frac{4}{3}),\\,(\\frac{4}{3},5),\\,(5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ | $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$ | $(\\frac{4}{3},5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $3x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)\\le 0$ on the intervals $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$, $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$, and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]⋃[1,\\frac{4}{3}]⋃[5,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186295216 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(3x-1)^{2}(2x+5)^{2}(4-x)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }2,\n$$ and $4.$ These roots divide the number line into five subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(\\frac{1}{3},2),\\,(2,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(3x-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(2x+5)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(4-x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(x-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is non-negative only on the closed interval $(2,4).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in {-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}}⋃[2,4].$"] [17592186295244 :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >4{x}^{5}+8{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4} & >0 \\\\ -4{x}^{5}-8{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+x+2) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2}(x+2)+(x+2)) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(-4{x}^{2}+1) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-4{x}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(1-2x)(1+2x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $1-2x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $1+2x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)⋃(-\\frac{1}{2},0)⋃(0,\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$"] [17592186297157 :question/worked-solution "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}=-1+\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}\n$$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $$\n\\displaystyle x=-1-\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}=\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}\n$$ is also a root."] [17592186297168 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $” is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"] [17592186297169 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=24\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\n$$ and $r=24.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the ball is decreasing at a rate of $16{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"] [17592186297239 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186297240 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186297242 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297243 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297244 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297245 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186297246 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186297247 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"]}
